# tests/assertions/multiline.rxt — `(?m)` ([M6.2] wave C;
# assertions_design.md §3.7, §10 wave C; D62, D63, D64).
#
# WHAT `(?m)` CHANGES, and it is exactly two assertions:
#
#   `(?m)^`  true at offset 0 OR immediately after a newline
#   `(?m)$`  true at end of subject OR immediately before a newline
#
# and NOTHING ELSE. `\A`, `\Z` and `\z` keep their absolute meanings under
# `(?m)` — PCRE2's own rule, and the reason src/parse/mod_assertions.c pins
# those nodes' multiline flag false rather than copying the scoped state the
# way `^`/`$` do. The blocks near the end are that pin's failing direction:
# if `\Z` ever started reading the flag, they go red.
#
# THE SCOPING IS THE POINT. `(?m)` is a SCOPED inline option, so the answer
# depends on the state in force AT EACH `^`/`$`, not on the pattern's final
# option state. `(?m:...)`, `(?m)...(?-m)` and a mid-pattern `(?m)` are all
# here, and they are the cells D47.5's own wording does not ask for (D62
# control 1) — the shipped pre-cure code got the leading-`(?m)` shape right
# and these wrong.
#
# ORACLES. Plain leading `(?m)` and `(?m:...)` are verifiable against BOTH
# python3 `re` and libpcre2, and every such cell here agrees on both. The
# other scoped spellings are NOT python-expressible at all — python rejects a
# bare `(?-m)`, a mid-pattern `(?m)` and a trailing `(?m)` outright ("global
# flags not at the start of the expression") — so those blocks carry
# `# pcre2-only` and ride tests/assertions/verify_pcre2.py, the convention
# wave A established for `\Z`. Every expectation in this file was PRODUCED by
# libpcre2 through tests/fuzz/pcre2_oracle; none was written by hand.

# ======================================================================
# 1. THE TWO ASSERTIONS, BARE. Zero-width, at every position of a subject
# set chosen so that newlines appear first, last, doubled and absent.
# ======================================================================

# pcre2-only
pattern (?m)^
features assertions,modifiers
m "" 0 0
m "a" 0 0
m "\n" 0 0
m "a\n" 0 0
m "\na" 0 0
m "ab" 0 0
m "a\nb" 0 0
m "ab\ncd" 0 0
m "a\n\nb" 0 0
m "\n\n" 0 0
m "abc\ndef\nghi" 0 0
m "a\nc" 0 0
m "\nc" 0 0
m "aaa\nbbb" 0 0
m "x\ny\nz" 0 0
m "b\na" 0 0
m "\nab\n" 0 0

pattern (?m)$
features assertions,modifiers
m "" 0 0
m "a" 1 1
m "\n" 0 0
m "a\n" 1 1
m "\na" 0 0
m "ab" 2 2
m "a\nb" 1 1
m "ab\ncd" 2 2
m "a\n\nb" 1 1
m "\n\n" 0 0
m "abc\ndef\nghi" 3 3
m "a\nc" 1 1
m "\nc" 0 0
m "aaa\nbbb" 3 3
m "x\ny\nz" 1 1
m "b\na" 1 1
m "\nab\n" 0 0

pattern ^
features assertions
m "" 0 0
m "a" 0 0
m "\n" 0 0
m "a\n" 0 0
m "\na" 0 0
m "ab" 0 0
m "a\nb" 0 0
m "ab\ncd" 0 0
m "a\n\nb" 0 0
m "\n\n" 0 0
m "abc\ndef\nghi" 0 0
m "a\nc" 0 0
m "\nc" 0 0
m "aaa\nbbb" 0 0
m "x\ny\nz" 0 0
m "b\na" 0 0
m "\nab\n" 0 0

pattern $
features assertions
m "" 0 0
m "a" 1 1
m "\n" 0 0
m "a\n" 1 1
m "\na" 2 2
m "ab" 2 2
m "a\nb" 3 3
m "ab\ncd" 5 5
m "a\n\nb" 4 4
m "\n\n" 1 1
m "abc\ndef\nghi" 11 11
m "a\nc" 3 3
m "\nc" 2 2
m "aaa\nbbb" 7 7
m "x\ny\nz" 5 5
m "b\na" 3 3
m "\nab\n" 3 3

