# ======================================================================
# 4b. THE SAME GATE CELLS, CAPTURE-BEARING — and this section is the one
# that can actually go red.
#
# POSSESSIFICATION IS A VM OPTIMIZATION. It removes backtracking states
# the VM would otherwise keep, and A DFA HAS NO BACKTRACKING TO REMOVE, so
# a capture-free `(?m)[^c]{1,3}$` routes to the DFA and answers correctly
# even with the gate's flag-read turned off. MEASURED: 749 find-all cells
# over the seven section-4 patterns, ZERO divergences under sabotage S77.
# Add one capturing group and the same pattern routes to the VM, where
# the miscompile is a LOST MATCH: `(?m)([^c]{1,3})$` on a subject whose
# line break separates the match from a `c` is (0,1) and becomes no
# match at all.
#
# So section 4 above pins the SEMANTICS on the engine most patterns take,
# and this section is D62's CONTROL 2 — the rows the permanent sabotage
# must turn red. Keeping both is deliberate: a reader who sees only the
# capture-bearing form would not know the capture is load-bearing, and a
# suite with only the capture-free form is what wave B's S75 came back
# UNDETECTED against ("every block in wordb.rxt was capture-free so
# nothing in the corpus reached emit_vm.c's arm at all").
# ======================================================================

pattern (?m)([^c]{1,3})$
features assertions,modifiers
n ""
m "a" 0 1
m "aa" 0 2
m "aaa" 0 3
m "aaaa" 1 4
m "ab" 0 2
n "abc"
m "a\n" 0 2
m "aa\n" 0 3
m "aaa\n" 0 3
n "\nc"
m "a\nc" 0 1
m "b" 0 1
m "ba" 0 2
n "c"
n "ac"
n "aac"
m "zaa" 0 3
m "zaaa\n" 1 4
m "\na" 0 2
m "a\nb" 0 3
m "ab\nc" 0 2
m "\n" 0 1
m "\n\n" 0 2
m "\n\nc" 0 1
m "a\nb\nc" 0 3
m "a\nb\nc\nd" 0 3
m "\nb\nc" 0 2

